BandC wrote:
>
> I play a male gnome healer lately and I kinda like to turn a boulder to
> meat and eat it. I become satiated and it takes a long long time to
> become hungry again which seems too good to be true. Is there a negative
> side to becoming satiated like becoming burdened?

Several.  The biggest disadvantage is the fact that you
will be Satiated most of the time but eating specific
corpses gets you important intrinsics.  Be satiated,
end up missing out on that corpse that would have been
likely to give you teleport control, or that other
corpse guaranteed to give you disintigration resistance.

> I don't totally understand the wishing concept. I only had to
> opportunity like twice I think. Now you have to type the name of an item
> "exactly" right? Do you waste your wish if you type an unknown item or
> misspell? If so, you have to know exactly what you are gonna wish. Isn't
> this kinda unfair for beginner players? I mean I don't know what to wish
> for. All I can think of is like: Iron skull cap +3.

The most exact the more likely you'll get what you
want within limits.

> My point is: wishing
> heavily relies on past experiences of the player. I know many other
> features also rely on past experiences of the player but this one more
> so. Can you explain wishing a little and what do you all think?

Actually, there is a great deal of sense in wishing for the
most usefull item you've gotten in any game so far.  Using
the exact spelling.  So if the best item you've ever gotten
so far is a "blessed +3 iron skull cap" then wish for that
with exactly that spelling.  If that is the best item you
have encountered in any of your games so far, it's actually
about as good a wish as you could make for your current
progress in the game.

Basically, I agree that the game depends on your experience.
That's the POINT.  If your character dies, you should write
down the lesson you learned from that character.  If you
don't know why you died (getting hit by a dozen orcs is NOT
why you died, getting into that situation is why you died)
you will not do better the next game.

This dependence on experience is why people still play
Nethack a decade later.  Nethack is chess/go/poker not the
latest flash in the pan video game that most will forget
in 5 years.



